PART 6  distinct eigenvalues: the quantity that predicts BOTH the
        circuit cost and the DLA.   TIER C - spectra only

  Wang (CQCWS1_19335 slide 9): an efficient circuit needs either
    (1) O(polylog N) DISTINCT eigenvalues, or
    (2) eigenvalues in closed form.
  Bridi et al. (2508.05749) Thm 1: dim(g_QWOA) <= m^2 + 1, m the
  number of DISTINCT eigenvalues of the problem Hamiltonian.
  Same quantity. Few eigenvalues buys a cheap circuit AND a
  collapsed DLA - cheap and useless together.

  graph          N distinct      route consequence               
  complete      16        2    (1) few small DLA, nothing to learn
  cycle         16        9 (2) closed LARGE DLA and still cheap 
  mobius        16        9 (2) closed LARGE DLA and still cheap 
  hypercube     16        5 (2) closed large DLA, cheap          
  complete      32        2    (1) few small DLA, nothing to learn
  cycle         32       17 (2) closed LARGE DLA and still cheap 
  mobius        32       17 (2) closed LARGE DLA and still cheap 
  hypercube     32        6 (2) closed large DLA, cheap          

  The complete graph's 2 eigenvalues are why PART 3 gave it S0 = 0
  and Phi_- localisation 0.50: there is no barrier and no structure.
  Bridi report DLA dimension exactly 2 for it.


PART 7  THE HYPERCUBE IS NOT A PARTICLE, IT IS A SPIN.
        TIER C - dense spectra of the reduced chain.

  PART 5's corrected Agmon metric did not fix the slope; it made it
  worse with n (-1.18 -> -2.72). The reason is structural, not a
  bad metric:  n*I - 2 J_x  is NOT the Laplacian of the reduced
  chain. Its row sums are

      n - sqrt(w(n-w+1)) - sqrt((w+1)(n-w))   !=  0

  which is n - sqrt(n) at the ends and ~0 in the middle - a large
  BUILT-IN inverted potential of size O(h^2 n) that no choice of
  Agmon metric on V alone can see.

  So the hypercube walk is a LARGE-SPIN system (Lipkin-Meshkov-Glick
  shaped), whose semiclassical parameter is 1/n, NOT h. Liu-Su-Li's
  framework is a particle with -h^2 Laplacian on R^d; sweeping h at
  fixed n is simply not its semiclassical limit.

  THE TEST. If the hypercube is a spin, then with the potential
  scaled EXTENSIVELY (V = n v(w/n)) and h FIXED, ln DeltaE should be
  linear in n with a constant slope -S~ (an intensive action).

  h fixed         n        DeltaE         ln dE   d lndE/dn
  Points are DISCARDED below the double-precision eigenvalue
  floor eps*||H||: a splitting of 1e-14 against ||H|| ~ 150 is
  LAPACK resolution, not physics. The first run of this part fitted
  through the floor and reported r = -0.58; the usable points had
  already given -0.959 and -0.970.

